// Accidentals are remembered per voice, where LilyPond remembers them per staff. In bar 1 the
// upper voice's fes' (its flat printed) and then the lower voice's f' at the same octave:
// LilyPond's default accidental style (the Accidental_engraver lives in Staff) prints a natural
// on that f', since the flat is in force on the staff; Lily# prints none, so the page shows a
// plain f' that reads as fes'. Bar 2 the other way: the upper voice's g' with its natural, then
// the lower voice's ges' (in the key): LilyPond prints the flat again, Lily# nothing, and the
// page reads as g'.
// Found by the OMR's grace note work (S21, 2026-10-18, gen-grace piano009 bar 1: the lower voice's
// grace { b16 a16 g16 } read as beses after the upper voice's beses8, its ges8 as g after the
// upper voice's acciaccatura { g8 }). The reading follows the staff-wide rule most engravers
// use; if Lily# means the per-voice rule (LilyPond's \accidentalStyle voice), the OMR cannot
// tell from the page which was meant.
// (lysc png this file: bar 1's lower f' has no natural, bar 2's lower ges' no flat)
octave absolute
time 4/4
key des major
part p1 { clef treble }
score { staff p1 }
section Piece {
  p1 {
    voice { fes''4 ges''4 aes''4 bes''4 } { des''4 ees''4 f''4 ges''4 } |
    voice { g''4 aes''4 bes''4 c'''4 } { des''4 ges''4 aes''4 bes''4 } |
  }
}
form { ~Piece }
